      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it from chemical, physical, and ecological perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Definition\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. \n* Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**.\n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties (Why Water is Unique)\nWater is famous for being weird. Unlike most substances, it has unique physical properties that make life possible:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or vapor).\n* **Universal Solvent:** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). Because of this, it acts as a carrier, transporting nutrients and chemicals through living organisms and ecosystems.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and get denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth’s surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans and seas.\n* **Distribution:** \n  * **96.5%** of Earth's water is salt water in the oceans.\n  * Only about **2.5%** is freshwater.\n  * Of that freshwater, most is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than **1%** is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and soil"
